WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.740
They're longtime rivals in the comics, but
Spider-Man failed to appear in Venom's solo

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:05.900
big-screen debut.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:06.580
Why?

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:07.940
Isn't this a Marvel movie?

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:08.980
Well, kinda.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.980
The answer's actually a lot more complicated
than that.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.060
If you want to know the real reason why Spider-Man's
not in the Venom movie, we've got to talk

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.040
some business.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.120
House divided

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.200
Marvel movies didn't really become "Marvel
movies" as you know them until 2008's Iron

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:23.700
Man.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:27.980
In the 1990s, Marvel's moviemaking division
was still trying to convince Hollywood that

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:32.300
movies and TV shows based on its comic books
might be something worth looking into.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.360
Since it had no direct leverage in the film
industry, Marvel farmed its properties out

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.420
to anyone who would take a chance on using
them.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.600
This is how Fox ended up making the X-Men
and Fantastic Four movies, how Universal ended

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.570
up in charge of the Hulk, and why Spider-Man
and his Amazing spider-friends ended up in

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:48.480
the hands of Sony.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.200
This is how things used to be.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.500
But the success of Iron Man and the birth
of Marvel Studios changed all that, getting

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.340
the mighty Marvel movie party in motion.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.059
Marvel Studios has since been acquired by
Disney, which more recently began the process

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:06.190
of acquiring Fox, which brings a large chunk
of the Marvel characters back under one roof,

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:07.880
except for Sony's spider-people.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.280
And that's where things get dicey.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:10.720
"None of this makes sense"

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.060
Unamazing Spider-plan

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.300
The same year that Marvel Studios took its
burgeoning cinematic universe to the next

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:24.300
level with The Avengers, Sony rebooted its
Spider-Man franchise with The Amazing Spider-Man.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.980
And Sony's plans didn't stop with Peter Parker.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.020
In an effort to compete with the rising MCU,
Sony began laying track for its own cinematic

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:34.850
universe set in the world of Spider-Man's
New York.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.220
During the production of Amazing Spider-Man
2, reports emerged that Sony was developing

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.980
a movie based on the villain team the Sinister
Six.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.220
There were also rumors of a potential Venom
spinoff, as well as a prequel movie focusing

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:51.270
on a young Aunt May, a mind-boggling proposition,
both then and also now.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:51.760
"Hey.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:52.440
Hey!

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.660
Hey, whaddaya doin, whaddaya doin, whaddaya
doin?

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:56.580
no, I do the laundry."

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.000
None of it panned out once the Amazing Spider-Man
sequel debuted to a franchise-low performance,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.840
leading Sony to re-evaluate its entire approach
to the Spider-Man property.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.880
In doing so, they leaned into a classic survival
strategy, if you can't beat 'em, join 'em.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:12.760
Homecoming

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.380
By 2014, Sony and Marvel were officially in
talks to collaborate on producing a second

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:23.540
Spider-Man reboot, talks which ultimately
culminated in 2017's Spider-Man: Homecoming.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.500
The team-up arrangement sported benefits for
both parties.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:31.720
Creatively, the arrangement allowed Marvel
Studios to use Spider-Man in its own MCU stories,

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.150
while Sony got to reap some profits off of
Marvel's golden touch.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:39.010
The deal has been reported to cover the production
of five movies total, with the fifth being

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.360
2019's Spider-Man: Far From Home.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:45.900
After that, no one's really sure what's going
to happen, either Sony will take back control

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.640
of Spider-Man, or the studios will continue
to share him.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.940
In the meantime, the finer details of who
gets to do what with which Marvel characters

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.260
can be kind of confusing for the rest of us.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.280
Muddying the waters

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.040
In 2017, Sony announced a standalone movie
for Venom, one of the most famous Marvel Comics

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.920
characters that it owns the rights to.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.570
The announcement almost immediately sparked
confusion about the movie's place in the MCU,

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:10.820
if it had a place at all.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.820
People wondered: Would Venom take place in
the same world as the Iron Man, Black Panther,

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.340
and the Guardians of the Galaxy?

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:18.940
Or would it be doing its own thing?

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.760
Even the people in charge of making the movies
couldn't get their story straight when talking

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:23.420
with the media.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.459
It was eventually cleared up that the MCU
would have nothing to do with the movies that

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:28.600
Sony made on its own.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.240
Basically, Marvel gets to handle the live-action
Spider-Man appearances, and everything Sony

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.900
does is Sony's business.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:36.800
Rumors abound

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.660
Sony began making the Venom movie with Tom
Hardy in the lead, drawing inspiration from

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.840
a comic that put the character in San Francisco.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:48.940
His costume was also altered to remove any
traces of Spider-Man's arachnid DNA.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.700
On top of that, the movie's director explicitly
said that Spider-Man would not be in the movie,

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.569
which should have been the end of the debate.

00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:58.340
But rumors still persisted that Spider-Man
would appear.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.320
One of the more pervasive rumors claimed that
current Spider-Man actor Tom Holland would

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.480
be appearing in Venom, just as Peter Parker,
and not suited up as Spider-Man.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.560
The rumor, most predominantly pushed by Collider's
Jon Schnepp, has a certain kind of logic to

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.060
it.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.620
After all, a Peter Parker appearance would
mean that all those denials of a Spider-Man

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.360
appearance would technically be correct.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.590
"You are Technically correct, the best kind of correct."

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.900
SUMC vs. MCU

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:28.240
Of course, as we all know now, neither Spider-Man,
Peter Parker, nor Tom Holland is anywhere

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.910
near the Venom movie as we saw it in theaters.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.480
Indeed, Venom is the start of something new,
which Sony plans to use as a springboard for

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.260
a new attempt at a cinematic universe.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.900
According to Variety, the series is being
internally referred to as "Sony's Universe

00:04:41.901 --> 00:04:46.930
of Marvel Characters", which is not a great
name, but hey, it's what we've got for now.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.280
The question is, who's next?

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.140
Sony is reportedly now in development on several
movies based on the Marvel Comics characters

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:57.460
to which it owns the film rights, a number
that Variety puts at around 900.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.300
Current movies in development include a Morbius,
the Living Vampire movie starring Jared Leto,

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:07.160
as well as movies based off of the characters
Jackpot, Black Cat, Kraven the Hunter, Nightwatch,

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.240
Silk, and Silver Sable, and those are just
the ones we know about.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.020
So will these movies actually come to fruition?

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:14.400
Probably not all of them.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.220
But the strong performance of Venom would
seem to put the studio in a good position

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.840
for the future, Spider-Man or no Spider-Man.

